ef 17592186125655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124737 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124737 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124737 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "feaa62a5-6a44-51be-9efd-89d2c7e4938d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2+6x-3y+xy$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=8.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(3,-6),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,8),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc6c7d4b-ed06-5426-80f2-415a147181e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125033 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/06/06660df896e67cef560398ca24217cdba2e2b9a34fe40f1829b3e1d5d2db24e3.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,-6)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,8),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,8).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,8) & =2+6(-2)-3(8)+(-2)(8) \\\\  & =-50 \\\\ f(1,2) & =2+6(1)-3(2)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,8) & =2+6(2)-3(8)+(2)(8) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-50$ at the point $(-2,8),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6$ at the point $(2,8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db6b4d4c-efe8-5d52-b8df-1c7481ef870b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125035 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(2,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+4 \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4 \\\\  & =13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4+0-8+4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0-12+4 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0+12+4 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the point $(-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14add615-dec7-5e1c-992f-2e9a1bbeb58c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125063 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e75a9f8c-78d9-58f9-8bfa-e3be31d2be7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125065 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-1 & x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y-1 &=0 \\\\ x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,1).$ Since the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨1,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+t⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1-t,2t) \\\\  & =(1-t)(2t)-(1-t) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}+3t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-2{t}^{2}+3t-1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -4t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125655 #edb/ref 1061 45426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85def1da-b072-51a0-ae08-591a0d352331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125656 #edb/ref 1061 45427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac225680-0726-58b8-b03b-f62ad289bb51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125657 #edb/ref 1061 45430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c1f6a3a-a0f9-5d34-85e4-3cbe4e6bca0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125658 #edb/ref 1061 45433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d1553dd-1fe4-5a34-9376-7547f1b2b500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125659 #edb/ref 1061 45434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccc80dce-cc5f-5576-95e2-9236c9281336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125892 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125892 #edb/ref 1065 7879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125892 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b8b379fb-f7e4-5cdb-b25a-69a6c2ae9cf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125892 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125893 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ and a closed and bounded region $D$ enclosed by several curves, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/04/042e4b736bdaa93033af34cc4ff738d1976a6595a448c8b697e717a74d19b67f.png)\n\nIn such cases, we need to search for extrema candidates among the following three categories:\n\nI. The critical points of $f$ that belong to the interior of $D.$\n\nII. The critical points of the function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}$ traverses each of the given parts of the boundary. In the example above, we would have to parametrize each of the $\\color{blue}4$ parts of the boundary separately.\n\nIII. The corner points of the boundary, i.e., the points where the different parts of the boundary meet. In the example above, we'd have to consider $\\color{red}4$ such points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17